<section xml:id="cmse381-lec07-evenmorelinreg-worksheet" component="ws07">
  <title>Day 07 Worksheet</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>In the last few lectures, we have focused on linear regression, that is, fitting models of the form</p>
    <p><md>
    Y =  \beta_0 +  \beta_1 X_1 +  \beta_2 X_2 + \cdots +  \beta_pX_p + \varepsilon
    </md></p>
    <p>In this lab, we will continue to use two different tools for linear regression.</p>
    <p><ul>
      <li>
        <p><url href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/index.html">Scikit learn</url> is arguably the most used tool for machine learning in python</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><url href="https://www.statsmodels.org">Statsmodels</url> provides many of the statisitcial tests we've been learning in class</p>
      </li>
    </ul></p>
    <p>This lab will cover two ideas:</p>
    <p><ul>
      <li>
        <p>Categorical variables and how to represent them as dummy variables.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>How to build interaction terms and pass them into your favorite model.</p>
      </li>
    </ul></p>
    <program language="python">
      # As always, we start with our favorite standard imports. 

      import numpy as np
      import pandas as pd
      import matplotlib.pyplot as plt 
      %matplotlib inline
      import seaborn as sns
      import statsmodels.formula.api as smf
    </program>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="cmse381-lec07-evenmorelinreg-worksheet-more-questions-to-ask-of-your-model-continued-from-last-time">
    <title>More questions to ask of your model (Continued from last time)</title>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="cmse381-lec07-evenmorelinreg-worksheet-q3-how-well-does-the-model-fit">
    <title>Q3: How well does the model fit?</title>
    <p>This section talks about interpretation of <m>R^2</m> and RSE, but is on slides only.</p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="cmse381-lec07-evenmorelinreg-worksheet-q4-making-predictions">
    <title>Q4: Making predictions</title>
    <program language="python">
      advertising_df = pd.read_csv('../../DataSets/Advertising.csv', index_col = 0)
      # I need to sort the rows by TV to make plotting work better later 
      advertising_df= advertising_df.sort_values(by=['TV'])
      advertising_df.head()
    </program>
    <program language="python">
      # I want to just learn Sales using TV
      est = smf.ols('Sales ~ TV', advertising_df).fit()
      est.params
    </program>
    <program language="python">
      # Here is a table giving us the CI and PI information 
      alpha = 0.1
      # alpha = 0.05
      # alpha = 0.01
      # alpha = 0.001
      advert_summary = est.get_prediction(advertising_df).summary_frame(alpha=alpha)
      advert_summary.head()
    </program>
    <program language="python">
      # And here is some code that will draw these beasts for us....
      plt.rcParams['figure.figsize'] = [10, 10]
      plt.rcParams['font.size'] = 16


      x = advertising_df['TV']
      y = advertising_df['Sales']


      # Plot the original data 
      plt.scatter(x,y, label = 'Data')

      # Plot the fitted values, AKA f_hat
      # you can get this in two different places, same answer
      # plt.plot(x,advert_summary['mean'], color = 'orange', label = 'f hat')
      plt.plot(x,est.fittedvalues, color = 'orange', label = 'f hat')



      plt.plot(x,advert_summary['obs_ci_lower'], 'r', lw=2, 
               label = r'Prediction Band')
      plt.plot(x,advert_summary['obs_ci_upper'], 'r', lw=2)

      plt.plot(x, advert_summary['mean_ci_lower'],'g--', lw=1, 
               label = r'Confidence Region')
      plt.plot(x, advert_summary['mean_ci_upper'], 'g--', lw=1)

      plt.title('Alpha = '+ str(alpha))



      plt.legend()
    </program>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="cmse381-lec07-evenmorelinreg-worksheet-playing-with-multi-level-variables">
    <title>Playing with multi-level variables</title>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="cmse381-lec07-evenmorelinreg-worksheet-the-wrong-way">
    <title>The wrong way</title>
    <p>Ok, we're going to do this incorrectly to start. You pull in the <c>Auto</c> data set. You were so proud of yourself for remembering to fix the problems with the <c>horsepower</c> column that you conveniently forgot that the column with information about country of origin (<c>origin</c>) has a bunch of integers in it, representing:</p>
    <p><ul>
      <li>
        <p>1: <c>American</c></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>2: <c>European</c></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>3: <c>Japanese</c>.</p>
      </li>
    </ul></p>
    <program language="python">
      Auto_df = pd.read_csv('../../DataSets/Auto.csv')
      Auto_df = Auto_df.replace('?', np.nan)
      Auto_df = Auto_df.dropna()
      Auto_df.horsepower = Auto_df.horsepower.astype('int')


      Auto_df.columns
    </program>
    <p>You then go on your merry way building the model
    <md>
    \texttt{mpg} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \cdot \texttt{origin}. 
    </md></p>
    <program language="python">
      from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression

      X = Auto_df.origin.values
      X = X.reshape(-1, 1)
      y = Auto_df.mpg.values

      regr = LinearRegression()

      regr.fit(X,y)

      print('beta_1 = ', regr.coef_[0])
      print('beta_0 = ', regr.intercept_)
    </program>
    <exercises>
      <title>Exercises</title>
      <exercisegroup>
        <title>Practice</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>Complete the following exercises.</p>
        </introduction>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>What does your model predict for each of the three types of cars?</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  # Predict mpg for origin codes 1, 2, and 3.
                  origin_codes = np.array([[1], [2], [3]])
                  ...
                </code>
              </program>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Use the fitted line <m>\widehat{mpg}=\widehat{\beta}_0+\widehat{\beta}_1\,origin</m> from the previous cell.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Substitute the numeric origin codes 1, 2, and 3, or pass a two-dimensional array containing those values to <c>regr.predict</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> The values 1, 2, and 3 are being treated as ordered numerical measurements here. That is exactly the limitation this example is intended to reveal.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>Using the coefficients printed by the preceding fit, the predictions are approximately 20.29 mpg for American cars, 25.77 mpg for European cars, and 31.24 mpg for Japanese cars.</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  origin_codes = np.array([[1], [2], [3]])
                  for name, prediction in zip(['American', 'European', 'Japanese'], regr.predict(origin_codes)):
                      print(f'{name}: {prediction:.2f} mpg')
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Is it possible for your model to predict that both American and Japanese cars have <c>mpg</c> below European cars?</p>
            </statement>
            <response/>
            <hint>
              <p>The fitted value is a straight-line function of the numeric code <c>origin</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Ask what the sign of the slope implies as the code moves from 1 to 2 to 3.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Do not treat the numeric ordering of the category codes as a meaningful scientific ordering; this is why the model specification is inappropriate.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>No. With one linear slope, the three fitted values must be monotone in the codes 1, 2, and 3 (or all equal if the slope is zero). The middle code therefore cannot be above both endpoint codes.</p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </exercisegroup>
    </exercises>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="cmse381-lec07-evenmorelinreg-worksheet-the-right-way">
    <title>The right way</title>
    <p>Ok, so you figure out your problem and decide to load in your data and fix the <c>origin</c> column to have names as entries.</p>
    <program language="python">
      convertOrigin= {1: 'American', 2:'European', 3:'Japanese'}

      # This command swaps out each number n for convertOrigin[n], making it one of
      # the three strings instead of an integer now.
      Auto_df.origin = Auto_df.origin.apply(lambda n: convertOrigin[n])
      Auto_df
    </program>
    <p>Below is a quick code that automatically generates our dummy variables. Yay for not having to code that mess ourselves!</p>
    <program language="python">
      origin_dummies_df = pd.get_dummies(Auto_df.origin, prefix='origin')
      origin_dummies_df
    </program>
    <p>I pass these new dummy variables into my <c>scikit-learn</c> linear regression model and get the following coefficients</p>
    <program language="python">
      X = origin_dummies_df.values
      y = Auto_df.mpg

      regr = LinearRegression()

      regr.fit(X,y)

      print('Coefs = ', regr.coef_)
      print('Intercept = ', regr.intercept_)
    </program>
    <exercises>
      <title>Exercises</title>
      <exercisegroup>
        <title>Practice</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>Complete the following exercises.</p>
        </introduction>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>What is the interpretation of each column in the <c>origin_dummies_df</c> data frame?</p>
            </statement>
            <response/>
            <hint>
              <p>Inspect one row of <c>origin_dummies_df</c> alongside the corresponding value in <c>Auto_df.origin</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Each dummy column answers a yes/no question about membership in one origin category.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> The dummy columns are indicators, not three new quantitative measurements; exactly one should be <c>True</c> for each car in this three-column encoding.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p><c>origin_American</c>, <c>origin_European</c>, and <c>origin_Japanese</c> are indicator variables. A column is <c>True</c> (or 1) exactly when the car belongs to that origin category and is <c>False</c> (or 0) otherwise; each row has exactly one <c>True</c>.</p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Now what does your model predict for each of the three types of cars?</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  # One-hot rows for American, European, and Japanese cars.
                  new_origins = np.array([[1, 0, 0],
                                          [0, 1, 0],
                                          [0, 0, 1]])
                  ...
                </code>
              </program>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Represent each origin by the matching one-hot row, keeping the columns in the same order used to fit <c>regr</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Pass all three rows to <c>regr.predict</c> in one call.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Including all three dummy columns together with an intercept creates an exactly redundant design. Predictions are still interpretable here, but the individual coefficients can be numerically unstable.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>The fitted category means are about 20.03 mpg for American cars, 27.60 mpg for European cars, and 30.45 mpg for Japanese cars. Small discrepancies in the displayed all-three-dummy fit can occur because that design is redundant with the intercept.</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  new_origins = np.array([[1, 0, 0],
                                          [0, 1, 0],
                                          [0, 0, 1]])
                  for name, prediction in zip(['American', 'European', 'Japanese'], regr.predict(new_origins)):
                      print(f'{name}: {prediction:.2f} mpg')
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Use only two dummy variables for the three-level <c>origin</c> variable, then refit the model.</p>
              <p><ul>
                <li>
                  <p>Are the coefficients different?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>Are the predictions for the three origins different?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>Does it matter which category is omitted?</p>
                </li>
              </ul></p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  # Use Japan as the reference category by keeping the other two columns.
                  X = origin_dummies_df[['origin_American', 'origin_European']].values
                  y = Auto_df.mpg

                  regr = LinearRegression()
                  regr.fit(X, y)

                  # Compare the three fitted category means.
                  ...
                </code>
              </program>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>With <c>origin_American</c> and <c>origin_European</c> retained, a Japanese car is represented by <c>(0, 0)</c> and becomes the reference category.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>The intercept is the reference-category mean; each slope is the difference between another category mean and the reference mean.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Changing the omitted category changes the coefficient interpretation, not the fitted value for each category. Keep the prediction-row order consistent with the retained columns.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>Using Japan as the reference gives an intercept of about 30.45, an American coefficient of about -10.42, and a European coefficient of about -2.85. The coefficients change when the reference category changes, but the fitted category means remain about 20.03, 27.60, and 30.45 mpg.</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  X = origin_dummies_df[['origin_American', 'origin_European']].values
                  y = Auto_df.mpg
                  regr = LinearRegression().fit(X, y)

                  print('Intercept:', regr.intercept_)
                  print('Coefficients:', regr.coef_)
                  rows = np.array([[1, 0], [0, 1], [0, 0]])
                  for name, prediction in zip(['American', 'European', 'Japanese'], regr.predict(rows)):
                      print(f'{name}: {prediction:.2f} mpg')
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </exercisegroup>
    </exercises>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="cmse381-lec07-evenmorelinreg-worksheet-another-right-way">
    <title>Another right way</title>
    <p>Ok, fine, I'll cave, I made you do it the hard way but you got to see how the innards worked, so maybe it's not all bad ;)</p>
    <p>First off, we can force <c>sklearn</c> to drop the first variable, so you don't have to do it manually every time. But you do need to know how to interpret the outputs!</p>
    <program language="python">
      # Even easier right way.... Note the only difference is the drop_first=True
      origin_dummies_df = pd.get_dummies(Auto_df.origin, prefix='origin',drop_first=True)
      print(origin_dummies_df.head())

      y = Auto_df.mpg
      regr = LinearRegression()
      regr.fit(X,y)
    </program>
    <p>In <c>statsmodels</c>, it can automatically split up the categorical variables in a data frame, so it does the hard work for you. Note that here I'm plugging in the original <c>Auto_df</c> data frame, no processing of the categorical variables on my end at all.</p>
    <program language="python">
      est = smf.ols('mpg ~ origin', Auto_df).fit()
      est.summary().tables[1]
    </program>
    <!-- horizontal rule in source notebook -->
    <exercises>
      <title>Exercises</title>
      <exercisegroup>
        <title>Practice</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>Complete the following exercises.</p>
        </introduction>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>What is the model learned from the above printout? Be specific in terms of your dummy variables.</p>
            </statement>
            <response/>
            <hint>
              <p>Statsmodels chooses one origin as the reference level and reports coefficients only for the other two levels.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>The intercept is the predicted mpg for the reference category; add a category coefficient to get that category's prediction.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Do not include a separate American term when American is the reference category; it is represented when both displayed dummy variables equal zero.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>With American as the reference category, the fitted model is <md>\widehat{mpg}=20.03+7.57\,I(origin=European)+10.42\,I(origin=Japanese).</md> The intercept is the American mean, and each slope is the difference from that mean.</p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </exercisegroup>
    </exercises>
  </subsection>
